## 1. Introduction
Along with the rapid development of industries such as electroplating, metallurgy and mining, heavy metal contamination has become a serious threat to ecosystems and human health. Differentiated from organic contaminants, metal pollutants are not biodegradable and tend to be accumulated in living organisms. Among the heavy metals, mercury ion $(\mathrm{Hg}^{2+})$ is considered as one of the most toxic metal ions. Excessive ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ can cause birth defects, brain damage and other serious diseases for humans and other organisms [1,2]. Because of the risk of chronic poisoning, the Minamata Convention and U.S. Environmental Protection Agency affirmed that $\mathrm{Hg}$ level in discharged wastewater cannot be more than 10 ppb [3]. So, highly efficient removal of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ from wastewater is urgently required.
In the past decades, many methods have been used for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ removal from wastewater, such as chemical precipitation, ion exchange, membrane filtration, liquid–liquid extraction and adsorption [4–7]. From the view of removal efficiency, reusability and applicability for low concentration, adsorption is considered as a comparatively efficient method. To date, many traditional materials have been reported to remove $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ from wastewater, such as activated carbon, zeolites and resins [8–11]. Nevertheless, it is still challenging in adsorption capacity, kinetics or selectivity. Thus, developing new classes of adsorbents is necessary to overcome these shortcomings.
Rapid development of porous materials provides new opportunity for adsorption technology. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), assembled by metal clusters and organic linkers, have attracted considerable attention in various application fields [12–16]. High porosity, adjustable channel structure, and abundant active sites make MOFs as ideal candidates for removing metal ions from wastewater [17–20]. Based on soft-hard acid-base theory, ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ can be easily captured by some sulfur-containing sites such as thiophene, thiol and disulfur units [21–23]. In the reported MOFbased adsorbents, sulfur units were commonly introduced via two following routes. On the one hand, sulfur-containing molecules were bonded to the uncoordinated metal sites of inorganic clusters [24–26]. On the other hand, some sulfur-containing ligands were selected as the linkers to assemble MOFs via in-situ or post-synthesis method [27–31]. In these MOFs, sulfur sites were either linked with metal clusters or presented in organic ligands. To the best of our knowledge, simultaneous utilization of organic ligand and inorganic cluster for sulfur sites introduction in MOFs was rarely reported up to now.
Herein, as a proof of the feasibility of this strategy, a novel dualsulfur DUT-67-SH was designed and successfully prepared. The primary DUT-67 contains the thiophene-based ligands and its $Z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ cluster is saturated by monodentate acetic acid (AA). In our work, AA molecule was substituted by thioglycolic acid (TA) to construct DUT-67-SH, via an in-situ green synthesis process (Scheme 1). The full use of inorganic cluster and organic ligand improves the density of active sulfur sites. The obtained materials were systematically characterized and the contents of sulfur sites were also determined. Benefited from improved chemical and structure property, DUT-67- SH exhibits high adsorption capacity and rapid adsorption kinetics. The effects of some factors such as temperature, pH, co-existing ion, and adsorbent dosage were further investigated. The corresponding adsorption mechanism was also cleared out.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Zirconium oxychloride octahydrate $(\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $98.0\ \%$ was purchased from J&K Scientific ltd. 2,5-Thiophenedicarboxylic acid ( $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TDC}}$ , $298.0\ \%$ ) was purchased from HWRK Chem. Thioglycolic
acid $(90.0\%)$ and acetic acid $(\geq99.5\%)$ were purchased from Macklin Biochemical Co. ltd. Deionized water was produced from our laboratory.
## 2.2. Preparation of DUT-67-SH and DUT-67-AA
Preparation of DUT-67-SH. In a $50~\mathrm{\mL}$ round-bottom flask, $\mathrm{{ZrOCl}}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{{H}}_{2}0$ $\cdot4\mathrm{~mmol}\dot{}.$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathrm{TDC}$ $2.66\ \mathrm{mmol}$ were well mixed in deionized water ( $\mathbf{\nabla}_{10\mathrm{mL}}$ . Then, TA (1, 3, 6, and $10\mathrm{mL}$ ) was added and stirred for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Subsequently, the mixture was refluxed at $95^{\circ}\mathrm{{^C}}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The white solid was collected through centrifugation and further washed with deionized water and methanol. At last, the solid was dried at $95~\mathrm{^circC}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The obtained materials were named as DUT-67-SH- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ ( $\mathbf{\dot{x}}$ is the usage of TA).
Preparation of DUT-67-AA. This material was synthesized based on the previous report [32].
## 2.3. Characterization techniques
The crystal topology was analyzed through powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern, based on an X-ray diffractometer (PANalytical). Surface morphology and elemental distribution were characterized by scanning electron microscope (SEM, TESCAN MIRA LMS) and energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS, Oxford). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo Scientific K-Alpha $^+$ ) was used to investigate the chemical state of elements during which Al $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\mathfrak{A}}$ was selected as the excitation source. Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR, Nicolet iS50) was employed to analyze the class of organic group. Nitrogen $(\mathsf{N}_{2})$ adsorption–desorption isotherms at 77 K were used to determine the specific surface area and pore structure, based on a PSD-PS2 surface area analyzer. $\mathrm{^1H}$ nuclear magnetic resource (NMR) spectra were collected on a Bruker AVANCE III $400~\mathrm{{MHz}}$ spectrometer. The sample for NMR measurement was prepared via dissolving MOF sample $\cdot10\ \mathrm{mg})$ in the solution of $\mathrm{H_{2}S O_{4}{-}}d_{6}/\mathrm{DMSO}d_{6}$ ( $0.5~\mathrm{mL}/1.0~\mathrm{mL}$ .
## 2.4. Adsorption experiments
The adsorption experiments were performed at batch mode in a constant temperature shaker. The solid-state adsorbent was added in the aqueous solution $\mathrm{.10~mL}$ , and the resulting mixture was shaken for the set time. The adsorbent usage is $5~\mathrm{mg}$ except for in the kinetics experiment $(20~\mathrm{mg})$ . It is noted that if no reminder, the pH of solution was not modified and kept the original value. The concentration of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ was determined by an inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES, AVIO 200). All tests were repeated two or three times. The detailed experimental conditions are given in the captions of the figures.
The adsorption amount (Q), distribution coefficient $\left(K_{\mathrm{d}}\right)$ and selectivity factor $(\alpha)$ were calculated by the Eqs. (1)–(3):
$$
\mathsf{Q}=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{e})V}{m}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{c}{{\displaystyle K_{d}=\frac{Q}{C_{e}}}}\\ {{\displaystyle\alpha=\frac{K_{d}^{H g}}{K_{d}^{M}}}}\end{array}
$$
where $C_{0}\ (\mathrm{mg/L})$ and $C_{\mathrm{e}}\ (\mathrm{mg/L})$ are the initial concentration and equilibrium concentration, respectively. $V(\mathrm{L})$ is the volume of solution, and $m\left({\mathfrak{g}}\right)$ is the usage amount of adsorbent.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization
The framework topologies of the prepared materials were first analyzed. As shown in Fig. 1a, the powder XRD patterns of DUT67-AA and DUT-67-SH are almost consistent with the simulated XRD data of DUT-67 crystal. The characteristic peaks at 6.7, 8.1, and $9.4^{\circ}$ for standard DUT-67 are clearly found. However, there appear several signs at 20.6 and $28.1^{\circ}$ in the pattern of DUT-67- SH-6, indicating that higher dosage of TA leads to the formation of additional crystal phases. Besides, excessive TA ( $10\mathrm{mL})$ was also attempted while no solid-state products were obtained even with double reaction time.
$\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms at $77~\mathrm{K}$ of the MOFs are shown in Fig. 1b. It is obvious that the curves of DUT-67-AA, DUT-67-SH-1 and DUT-67-SH-3 belong to the type-I isotherm. ${\sf N}_{2}$ uptake amount is sharply increased at $P/P_{0}{<}0.1$ , confirming the microporous structure of the three MOFs. DUT-67-SH-6 exhibits the obviously different curves and very low $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption amount at low pressure range, excluding the existence of micorpores. The pore parameters of the four MOFs are listed in Table 1. Among the materials, DUT-67-SH-3 possesses the largest specific surface area of $939.9\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Compared with AA, the introduction of TA with larger molecular size increases the defect units (discussed below), beneficial for boosting porosity [33,34]. However, due to the smaller amount of acid modifier (TA), the crystallinity of DUT-67-SH-1 is obviously lower than that of DUT-67-SH-3. Besides, in the preparation of DUT-67-SH-6, we observed the formation of the solid-state products was very slow and product yield was very low, even with longer synthesis time. That is, upon the addition of excess acid modifier, the assembling of $Z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ cluster and TDC linker is limited. Besides, the excess acid also induces the formation of some unknown phases. These lead to the worst porosity of DUT-67-SH-6. Furthermore, based on NLDFT method, the pore diameters of DUT-67-AA, DUT-67-SH-1 and DUT-67-SH3 were determined to 0.58, 0.77 and $0.75~\mathrm{\nm}$ , respectively (Fig. S1). The slight increase of pore size may be attributed to the formation of defects caused by TA molecules.
The FTIR spectra of the samples are presented in Fig. 2. The coordination of the organic ligand and $Z\mathrm{r}–0$ cluster is confirmed by the dual peaks at $\sim1527$ and $1389\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which are attributed to the asymmetric and symmetric vibrations of carboxylate (COO-Zr) [35]. The presence of -SH in DUT-67-SH samples iss verified by the weak peaks at $\sim2560\ c m^{-1}$ [36,37]; as a comparison, this peak cannot be found in the spectrum of DUT-67-AA. Besides, the peak at $\sim800.5~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to $c{-}s$ bonds [38,39] which may belong to thiophene or thiol units. These results indicate that -SH groups have been introduced successfully.
The content of -SH in the materials was determined based on SEM-EDS and $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR measurement. As proved in the literatures [40,41], the standard formula of DUT-67-acid is $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{6}(O H)_{2}(T D C)_{4}(a c i d)_{x},}$ and thereby the theoretical molar ratio of $S/Z\mathrm{r}$ is 0.67. As listed in Table 1, the $S/Z\mathrm{r}$ ratio of assynthesized DUT-67-AA is 0.61 and its formula can be determined to $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{6}(O H)_{2}(T D C)_{3.7}(A A)_{x}}$ The missing linker is attributed to the formation of defects. Similarly, DUT-67-SH can be roughly defined as $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{6}(O H)_{2}(T D C)_{x}(T A)_{y}}$ . As shown in Fig. S2, the signs of TDC and TA are clearly found in the $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectra of DUT-67-SH-1 and DUT-67-SH-3, and the molar ratios of TA/TDC in the two MOFs were determined to 1.06 and 1.19. Combining the $S/Z\mathrm{r}$ ratio and TA/TDC ratio, we speculate the formulas of the two MOFs are $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ - $\mathrm{O}_{6}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{TDC})_{2}(\mathrm{TA})_{2.1}$ and $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{6}(O H)_{2}(T D C)_{2.5}(T A)_{3.0}}$ respectively. On the basis, the contents of -SH were calculated to 1.74 and $2.19\mathrm{\mmol/g}$ for DUT-67-SH-1 and DUT-67-SH-3. The case of DUT-67-SH-6 was not considered due to the presence of unknown phase and bad porosity.
Furthermore, SEM image was used to observe the morphology of the samples. As shown in Fig. 3a, DUT-67-AA consists of some irregular particles with the size of $\sim100{-}300\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The particle shape and size of DUT-67-SH samples depend on the addition amount of TA. DUT-67-SH-1 shows irregular particles with the homogenous size of $\sim100\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 3b). The particle of DUT-67- SH-3 tends to be relatively regular octahedron with higher size (Fig. 3c). For DUT-67-SH-6, besides the large polyhedrons, some small irregular particles $(\sim50\ \mathrm{nm})$ are also clearly found (Fig. 3d), which may be assigned to the unknown phases.
## 3.2. $H g^{2+}$ adsorption
## 3.2.1. Screening and comparison for $H g^{2+}$ adsorption
To evaluate the adsorption performances of the prepared materials as well as the feasibility of our design strategy, the adsorption isotherms at the initial concentration range of $10{-}1000~\mathrm{mg/L}$ were measured (Fig. 4a). The isotherm of DUT-67-AA belongs to the ‘‘S” type: the adsorption amounts at lower concentrations $\mathrm{10-50~mg/}$ L) are very low due to the weaker affinity of thiophene towards ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ (Fig. S3); with the increasing concentration, the adsorption is enhanced and tends to reach equilibrium, during which the large concentration difference acts as the dominant driven force for the adsorption. Different from that, the -SH-modified samples especially for DUT-67-SH-1 and DUT-67-SH-3 can almost capture all the ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ ions at the low concentrations, suggesting the stronger affinity of -SH towards ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ (than thiophene). Among the four samples, DUT-67-SH-3 exhibits the largest adsorption capacity of $372.0\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Compared with DUT-67-SH-3, the particle sizes of
DUT-67-AA and DUT-67-SH-1 are smaller and thereby larger outer surface areas can be exposed to guest $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in theory. As a result, the diffusion and mass transfer of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ from solution to adsorbent surface may be boosted. However, less thiol groups and lower specific surface areas lead to their lower adsorption amounts. For DUT-67-SH-6, even with higher $S/Z\mathrm{r}$ ratio (1.34), the very low specific surface area sharply limits the diffusion and mass transfer of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ . Besides, a comparison with some other MOF-based adsorbents was carried out and the adsorption parameters were listed in Table S1. It is clear that DUT-67-SH-3 is superior or comparable with those adsorbents.
Further, the adsorption data was fitted by the classical isotherm models to understand the adsorption behaviour of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ,
Langmuir isotherm equation:
$$
\frac{C_{e}}{Q_{e}}=\frac{1}{K_{L}Q_{m}}+\frac{C_{e}}{Q_{m}}
$$
Freundlich isotherm equation:
$$
\ln Q_{e}=l n K_{F}+\frac{1}{n}l n C_{e}
$$
where $K_{\mathrm{L}}$ and $Q_{\mathrm{m}}$ are the adsorption coefficient and theoretical equilibrium adsorption capacity for Langmuir model, respectively; KF and $1/n$ are the parameters related to adsorption capacity and intensity for Freundlich model, respectively. The fitting curves and parameters are shown in Fig. 4b and Table 2. The adsorption of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in DUT-67-AA cannot be well described by the two models, due to its ‘ $\cdot\boldsymbol{S}^{\prime}$ -type isotherm. The adsorption data of all the DUT67-SH materials are better fitted by Langmuir model than Freundlich model, indicating the monolayer adsorption mode.
As discussed above, DUT-67-SH-3 owns the highest adsorption capacity and thereby its detailed adsorption behaviour for ${\mathrm{\dot{H}g^{2+}}}$ was investigated as follows.
3.2.2. Effect of adsorbent dosage and adsorption kinetics of DUT-67- SH-3
Removal efficiency is one of pivotal indicators to evaluate adsorbent performance. Herein, we firstly investigated the ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ removal efficiency under various adsorbent dosages. As shown in Fig. 5a, with the increase of adsorbent usage, the removal ratio of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ is gradually increased and tends to reach equilibrium. Almost complete removal $(99.9\%)$ is achieved under the adsorbent dosage of $2.0~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ .
Further, the adsorption speed was evaluated under this optimized dosage. As presented in Fig. 5b, fast adsorption happens at 1 min at which the adsorption amount reaches $91.8\%$ of the largest adsorption amount. Adsorption equilibrium is achieved at $3\mathrm{min}$ at which the removal ratio of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ reaches $99.5\mathrm{~\%~}$ . Thus, the fast adsorption kinetics and high removal efficiency of DUT-67-SH-3 are proved. Furthermore, the adsorption kinetics of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in DUT67-SH-3 was understood through data fitting with pseudo-firstorder and pseudo-second-order models,
pseudo-first-order model:
$$
\ln{(Q_{e}-Q_{t})}=l n Q_{e}-k_{1}t
$$
pseudo-second-order model:
$$
\frac{t}{\mathrm{Q}_{t}}=\frac{1}{k_{2}\mathrm{Q}_{e}^{2}}+\frac{t}{\mathrm{Q}_{e}}
$$
where $Q_{\mathrm{t}}$ is the adsorption amount at time t, $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are rate constants of pseudo-first/second order models, respectively. From the fitting parameters in Table 3, pseudo-second-order model can better describe the adsorption kinetics behaviour of DUT-67-SH-3. This indicates that chemical interaction may dominate the ratecontrolling step of the adsorption process, which may be derived from the strong bonding interaction of sulfur sites and $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ .
## 3.2.3. Adsorption thermodynamics
The effect of temperature on ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ adsorption was studied based on the experiments at 303, 313 and $323\mathrm{K}.$ As shown in Fig. 6a, the adsorption of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ is enhanced with the increasing temperature and the adsorption capacities at 303, 313 and $323\mathrm{~K~}$ reach 372.0, 404.1 and $423.6\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Further, the adsorption thermodynamic behavior was studied in detail. The thermodynamic parameters including enthalpy change $(\Delta H)$ , entropy change $(\Delta S)$ and Gibbs free energy change $(\Delta G)$ were calculated by Eqs. (8) and (9):
$$
\ln{\frac{Q_{e}}{C_{e}}}=-{\frac{\Delta H}{R T}}+{\frac{\Delta S}{R}}
$$
$$
\Delta G=\Delta H-T\Delta S
$$
Based on the fitting curve of $\ln(Q_{\mathrm{e}}/C_{\mathrm{e}})$ and $1/T$ (Fig. 6b), the value of $\Delta H$ was determined to $4.76~\mathrm{kJ}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ (Table 4), indicating the endothermic nature of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ adsorption process. The positive value of $\Delta S$ suggests the increased freedom degree, attributed to the release of the water molecules of hydrated mercury ions during the adsorption process. The negative $\Delta G$ values prove that the adsorption of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in DUT-67-SH-3 is spontaneous. Meanwhile, the gradually increased $\Delta G$ values from 303 to $323\mathrm{~K~}$ suggest that higher temperature can facilitate the adsorption.
## 3.2.4. Selective adsorption
Practical wastewater commonly contains several co-existing inorganic salts. To investigate the potential of DUT-67-SH-3 for treating real water, the selective adsorption performance for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in a multi-ion solution was measured. As displayed in Fig. 7a, even with the interferences from other cations, the removal percentage of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ can still reach up to $99.0\%$ . As a comparison, DUT-67-SH-3 can hardly adsorb ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ , $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ , $C0^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ . The removal percentages for these co-existing cations are only 0.1, 0.1, 2.8, 1.4, and $1.5\%$ , respectively. This indicates that DUT-67-SH-3 owns stronger affinity towards $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ than other cations.
The selective adsorption ability was further evaluated through distribution coefficients $\left(K_{\mathrm{d}}\right)$ calculation. In general, the $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ value of an excellent adsorbent should be larger than $1.0\times10^{4}\mathrm{~mL~g}^{-1}$ . As presented in Fig. 7b, the $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ reaches $1.4\times10^{5}\mathrm{~mL~g}^{-1}$ and that for other co-existing cations are all lower than $60{\mathrm{~mL~g}}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the separation factors $(\alpha)$ were determined according to the values of $K_{\mathrm{d}}$ (Fig. 7c). The values of $\alpha$ for the monovalent ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{K}}^{+}$ ions are close to $7.0\times10^{5}$ and that for other divalent and trivalent ions are also relatively high $(2.0\times10^{3}\sim5.0\times10^{3})$ .
On the basis, the application of DUT-67-SH-3 to real FGD (flue gas desulfurization) wastewater was validated. The wastewater was prepared according to Heidel et al. work [42] and the composition was listed in Table S2. Through a batch adsorption treatment (adsorbent dosage, $0.5~\mathrm{g/L}\rangle$ , the concentration of ${\mathrm{Hg}}({\mathrm{II}})$ decreases from $0.251~\mathrm{mg/L}$ $:1.25\times10^{-6}\mathrm{\mol/L})$ to $0.01\ \mathrm{mg/L},$ arriving at the standard of the Minamata Convention and U.S. Environmental Protection Agency. The removal percentage of ${\mathrm{Hg}}({\mathrm{II}})$ reaches $\sim96$ . $0\%$ , indicating the excellent removal performance of DUT-67-SH-3. Thus, this material may be used in treatment of real wastewater.
## 3.2.5. Effect of solution $p H$
The solution pH commonly affects the adsorption process by controlling the chemical states of adsorbent and adsorbate. Herein, the adsorption experiments were performed at $\mathfrak{p H}$ range of $2.0~\sim~7.0$ . As shown in Fig. 8, the optimized pH locates at $4.0\sim6.0$ and slight decreases happen at $\mathrm{pH}<4.0$ and greater than 6.0. To understand this, the zero potential point $(\mathsf{p H}_{\mathsf{z p}})$ of the adsorbent was measured (Fig. S4). At $\mathsf{p H}=4$ , the surface charge of the MOF is close to neutral and the electrostatic attraction interaction towards cationic ${\mathrm{Hg}}({\mathrm{II}})$ ion is much weaker. At $\mathsf{p H}=2-3$ , the MOF surface is positively charged and thereby an electrostatic repulsion happens. Meanwhile, lower pH stands for larger amount of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ , which will compete with cationic ${\mathrm{Hg}}({\mathrm{II}})$ ion. At higher pH of
7.0, mercury ion tends to be hydrolyzed to form ${\mathrm{Hg}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ species and thereby the size of mercury(II) is largely increased [26]. As a result, the diffusion and adsorption of ${\mathrm{Hg}}({\mathrm{II}})$ into DUT-67-SH is reduced slightly. However, in the whole acidic range, the removal ratios of ${\mathrm{Hg}}({\mathrm{II}})$ in DUT-67-SH-3 are all larger than $92.0\%$ This indicates that DUT-67-SH-3 has excellent applicability in the acidic range.
## 3.2.6. Regeneration of the adsorbent
In this work, we used the solvent elution method to regenerate the $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ -loaded material. Firstly, three classes of commonly used eluents were evaluated and screened, including thiourea solution, thiourea/HCl solution, and EDTA-2Na solution. As shown in Fig. 9a, after the treatment by EDTA-2Na solution, the removal ratio of the regenerated sample reaches up to $99.1\ \%$ , very close to that of the fresh sample. We suggest the strong chelation ability of EDTA contributes largely to the elution of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ . As a comparison, the elution ability of thiourea is relatively poorer and the addition of HCl seems to improper due to the possible running off of active sites. On the basis, we further investigated the regeneration ability of EDTA-2Na solution in detail. As presented in Fig. 9b, the value can still reach $\sim92.0\%$ after three adsorption–desorption cycles. Hence, DUT-67-SH-3 may serve as a regenerable adsorbent for $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ removal.
## 3.2.7. Adsorption mechanism study
Firstly, the stability of DUT-67-SH-3 towards $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ adsorption was confirmed. As shown in Fig. 10a, although the intensities of some diffraction peaks are changed, almost all the diffraction peaks remain and no new peaks are found after $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ adsorption. Besides, the SEM image (Fig. 10b) reflects that the morphology of MOF particle is not destroyed. Further, the SEM-EDS mapping was used to observe the elemental distribution. As shown in Fig. 10c-f, the sign of $\mathrm{Hg}$ can be clearly found and its distribution is consistent with that of S and $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ . This indicates that $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ is uniformly adsorbed on DUT-67-SH-3.
Further, the XPS patterns of DUT-67-SH-3 before and after adsorbing $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ were collected. As presented in Fig. 11a, the characteristic signs of Hg 4f can be clearly found. In the high-resolution XPS pattern (Fig. 11b), the peaks for $\mathrm{Hg~}4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}$ and $4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ locate at 104.9 and $100.9\mathrm{~eV}$ and the deviation value is $4.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ . The binding energies of the two peaks are found to be very close to that of $\operatorname{Hg-}S$ compound [43,44], which may prove the chemical binding of S sites and $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ . Furthermore, the high-resolution $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}$ peaks were analyzed. As shown in Fig. 11c and d, after ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ adsorption, the overall peak shape of $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}$ experiences a large change. The areas of $c{-}s$ bonds for thiophene and thiol are largely decreased and two additional peaks appear at lower locations, which are assigned to $S{\mathrm{-Hg}}$ bonds [45]. Thus, combined above, the bonding interaction between S sites and $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ is confirmed.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, a novel MOF-based adsorbent containing dual classes of sulfurs was successfully synthesized. For the first time, thiophene and thiol sites were simultaneously introduced into MOFs, via an in-situ green method. Benefited from high-density active sites and strong soft acid-base interaction, the prepared DUT-67-SH-3 exhibits a large adsorption capacity of $370.2\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for ${\mathrm{Hg}}^{2+}$ , far higher than that of SH-free DUT-67 and other reported adsorbents. Besides, this MOF owns a fast adsorption equilibrium time of $\sim3\ \mathrm{min}$ and high distribution coefficient of $1.4\times10^{5}\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Meanwhile, the adsorption is not largely affected by coexisting cations and solution pH (acidic range). This material was also found to be regenerable via EDTA elution method. Thus, this work highlights the feasibility of acid-substitution method for constructing novel adsorbents with high-density sulfur sites.
